-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/eeedbb8d-0250-53f0-a6f2-f59cadf07483
-- title:
--   Descent of Uₚ and ⟨ d⟩ to torus and Raynaud quotients
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a modulus $M$ together with a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, and assume $p \mid M$ (`hpM`), $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), and that $H$ contains every unit of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ whose image under `ZMod.unitsMap` for $M/p \mid M$ is trivial (`hHp`). Fix a valuation subring $Pl$ of a fixed algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $Pl$ (`hPl`, the predicate `LiesOverPrime p`), whose residue field is of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed, and fix the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`.
--
--   **Geometric frame.** $\mathfrak{X}$ is an integral model datum `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` for the two-chart models `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` and `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` over the base ring `R p`, $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl`, and $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, so $O$ provides a smooth separated commutative group scheme $g : O.G \to$ `base p` with a relative group law $O.L$, an identification `O.pts` of $J_H =$ `JH M H` (the degree-zero divisor class group `Pic0` of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$) with the generic sections of $g$, Hecke endomorphisms `O.hecke S t` for the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13), and a toric rank `O.toricRank`. The hypotheses `hrep` and `hrepΛ` assert that the relative Picard subfunctor cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero (`algEquivZeroCut`) is represented: for the model of level `ΓM M H` rigidified along the section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_\infty$ by the designation whose total space is $(O.G, O.g)$ with zero section the unit of $O.L$, and for the model of level `ΓN p M H hpM` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_\infty$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$ by the designation $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ with zero section the unit of $\Lambda.L$; in each case representability means the existence of a rigidified Poincaré bundle satisfying the condition, the usual universal property for rigidified line bundles satisfying the condition, and triviality of the pullback along the zero section.
--
--   **Henselian frame.** $R_h$ is a henselian local domain with an algebra structure on the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$ for which the action is faithful, such that $R_h$ maps into $Pl$ (`hRA`) and its maximal ideal consists exactly of the elements of valuation $< 1$ (`hRloc`); moreover $R_h$ carries a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra structure whose kernel is described by the same valuation condition (`hres`). A set of naturals $S$ and a unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ are fixed, to index the Hecke generators used below.
--
--   **The witness $(\mathcal{G}, \Delta, e)$.** $\mathcal{G}$ is a $p$-divisible group over $R_h$ of height $h$ in the Hopf-algebraic sense (levels `𝒢.level v` finite free cocommutative Hopf $R_h$-algebras of rank $p^{vh}$, surjective transition maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals), $\Delta$ is an additive map from its group of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-points to $J_H$, and $e$ is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map of Tate modules, where [`TateModule p A`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) consists of the sequences $(x_n)$ in $A$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p x_{n+1} = x_n$. The hypotheses on them are: $\Delta$ is injective (`hΔinj`); `hΔlev` identifies `O.finPts (p ^ v)` (the subgroup generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes of `Pic0` that extend to the place) with the image under $\Delta$ of the level-$v$ points; `hΔgal` makes $\Delta$ equivariant for any $R_h$-automorphism of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ and the $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism it induces; `hΔhecke` provides, for every $S$ and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13), a transition-compatible family of bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels of $\mathcal{G}$ whose effect on points corresponds under $\Delta$ to the Hecke operator `genOpH M H S g`; `he` says that $e$ is given termwise by $\Delta$; `heinj` that $e$ is injective; `herange` that a Tate vector lies in the range of $e$ exactly when its $n$-th term lies in `O.finPts (p ^ n)` for all $n$; `hegal` that $e$ intertwines the Tate module representation of $\mathcal{G}$ with `JH.tateGaloisRep`; `hsat` that the range of $e$ is saturated ($p \cdot y$ in the range forces $y$ in the range); `hcoker` that the cokernel of $e$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linearly isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$; and `htor` that every element of `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point.
--
--   **The sub-object $\mathcal{B}$ (toric part).** $\mathcal{B}$ is a $p$-divisible group over $R_h$ of height $hB$ with $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + hB$ (`hhB`) and $hB = 2h'$ (`hhB2`), together with a family $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of $R_h$-bialgebra maps compatible with the transitions (`hψt`). Further: `hψker` says that, for a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal{G}$, the $\mathcal{B}$-point obtained by composing $x$ with $\psi_v$ is trivial precisely when $\Delta(x)$ lies in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`; `hψsurj` that every $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $\mathcal{B}$ at level $v$ arises this way from a point of $\mathcal{G}$; `hψred` that if the $\psi_v$-image of $x$ reduces to the identity, in the sense that $a \mapsto x(\psi_v(a))$ differs from the counit by elements of valuation $< 1$, then $x$ itself reduces to the identity in the same sense; and `hperiod` that for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ inside $\mathrm{Gal}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ (the predicate `inertiaSubgroupIn`), every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ of `Pic0` and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi_v$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity.
--
--   **Pinning of $\mathcal{G}$ inside $O.G$.** A ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to R_h$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ are given, subject to: `hρh`, compatibility of $\rho_h$ with the algebra maps to $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hιbase`, that $\iota_v$ lies over $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$; `hιcl`, that the induced morphism into the relevant fibre product is a closed immersion; `hιp`, that $\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^v$ is the unit section; `hιpts`, that the section attached by `O.pts` to $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point $x$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$; `hιmul`, that $\iota_v$ is multiplicative for the group law $O.L$ on points with values in any $R_h$-algebra $B$; `hιt`, compatibility of the $\iota_v$ with the transition maps; `hιhecke`, that each Hecke generator is induced, along $\iota$, by a transition-compatible family of bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels acting on points as `genOpH M H S g` does through $\Delta$; and `hιfin`, that the comparison morphism $j_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ into the base change along $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$ of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and that every point of that base change lying over the closed point of $R_h$ is in the image of $j_v$.
--
--   **The two operators.** $u_v$ is a family of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels of $\mathcal{G}$, compatible with the transitions (`hut`) and inducing along $\iota$ the Hecke endomorphism `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.U p Fact.out hpM)` (`huι`); $D_{p,v}$ is a family of $R_h$-bialgebra automorphisms of the levels, compatible with the transitions (`hDpt`) and inducing along $\iota$ the diamond endomorphism `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.dia d)` (`hDpι`). Both are assumed to preserve toric points read through $\Delta$: if $\Delta(x) \in$ `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` then the same holds for the point obtained from $x$ by composing with $u_v$ (`hutor`), respectively with $D_{p,v}$ (`hDptor`).
--
--   **The Raynaud quotient datum.** `hjB` assumes that the base change $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \psi_v$ of each $\psi_v$ is injective. Finally $T_v$ is a family of commutative rings that are finite free cocommutative Hopf algebras over $\mathbb{Z}/p$, equipped with surjective $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bialgebra maps $\pi^T_v : \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to T_v$ (`hπT`) whose kernel is exactly the ideal generated by the image under $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes \psi_v$ of the augmentation ideal of $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, that is, of the kernel of its counit (`hexT`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist families $u^T_v, e^T_v : T_v \to T_v$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bialgebra endomorphisms, a family $u^B_v$ of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels of $\mathcal{B}$, a family $D^B_v$ of $R_h$-bialgebra automorphisms of those levels, and a family $D^{B\prime}_v$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bialgebra automorphisms of $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, such that for every $v$:
--
--   1. $\pi^T_v \circ (\mathrm{id} \otimes u_v) = u^T_v \circ \pi^T_v$;
--
--   2. $\pi^T_v \circ (\mathrm{id} \otimes D_{p,v}) = e^T_v \circ \pi^T_v$;
--
--   3. $u_v \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ u^B_v$;
--
--   4. $D_{p,v} \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ D^B_v$;
--
--   5. $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}_v \circ u^B_{v+1} = u^B_v \circ \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}_v$;
--
--   6. $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}_v \circ D^B_{v+1} = D^B_v \circ \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}_v$;
--
--   7. $D^{B\prime}_v$, viewed as a bialgebra map, is the base change $\mathrm{id} \otimes D^B_v$;
--
--   8. $(\mathrm{id} \otimes u_v) \circ (\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v) = (\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v) \circ (\mathrm{id} \otimes u^B_v)$;
--
--   9. $(\mathrm{id} \otimes D_{p,v}) \circ (\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v) = (\mathrm{id} \otimes \psi_v) \circ D^{B\prime}_v$,
--
--   where $\mathrm{id} \otimes (-)$ denotes the base change along $R_h \to \mathbb{Z}/p$ given by `Bialgebra.TensorProduct.map (BialgHom.id (ZMod p) (ZMod p))`. No further property of $u^T$, $e^T$, $u^B$, $D^B$, $D^{B\prime}$ is asserted.
--
--   This is the descent step for the operators $U_p$ and $\langle d\rangle$ in the analysis of the $p$-divisible group attached to the finite part of the Néron object of $J_H(M)$ at $p$: the two operators on $\mathcal{G}$, assumed to preserve the toric points, are shown to descend both to the sub-object $\mathcal{B}$ cut out by $\psi$ and to the Raynaud-type quotients $T_v$ modulo $p$, compatibly with base change to $\mathbb{Z}/p$. It is combined with the component-group and Eichler–Shimura ingredients in [`ModularCurve.exists_twoStepTower_raynaudQuotient_descent_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_twoStepTower_raynaudQuotient_descent_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins), on the way to the level-lowering input at the prime $p$ with $p \| M$.
